NAME
	video-pane (display) - render a decoded video feed from a vid9p stream directory, compositing I420 frames through the mpeg remap24 path into the pane, with the layers bound into the feed's layer/ directory drawn over them

INTERFACE
	display (MatrixDisplay)

READS
	<mount>/fmt	"<w> <h> i420 <fps>" — stream geometry.
	<mount>/frame	the I420 byte stream; each w*h*3/2 bytes is one
			decoded frame (see appl/cmd/vid9p.b).

WRITES
	none (renders only)

	<mount>/layer/	read: each file a layer of records drawn over the
			frames, stacked in the union's order (layers(2))

DESCRIPTION
	The mount path is a vid9p stream directory (e.g. /mnt/video/0)
	served by appl/cmd/vid9p.b over the host-decode 9P bridge
	(docs/H264-9P-BRIDGE.md).  fmt is read once for geometry; frames
	are fetched by index with pread (the frame file is random
	access), wrapped as a Mpegio->YCbCr and run through the SAME
	remap24 (YCbCr->RGB24) converter the standalone vidplay/vidplay9p
	players use — there is no new pixel code.  The frame is drawn at
	its native size, centred in the pane and clipped to the pane
	rectangle, with a transport status strip along the bottom edge.

	The pane is a transport-controlled player.  It exports the
	optional MatrixTicker interface (matrix.m), so the runtime calls
	update() at frame cadence; playback paces itself against the
	wall clock at the feed's fps.  Keys, routed by Matrix to the
	focused (hovered) pane:

	space	play / pause
	s	stop: canned feed rewinds to frame 0, paused; a live
		feed snaps back to the live edge, playing
	arrows	seek 5 s back / forward; on a live feed, seeking back
		replays the retained buffer (DVR) and seeking to the
		end resumes following the live edge

	A canned clip loops at end of stream; a live (streaming) feed
	follows the newest decoded frame.  fmt/frame need not exist at
	init: the pane retries lazily and shows "no video" until the
	source appears.  Multiplex by placing one video-pane leaf per
	feed in the composition (see the video-player and video-live
	crystallisations in /lib/matrix/compositions).

	LIVE SEQUENTIAL SOURCES.  A mount whose status carries no
	transport (no "pos=" field: camsrv(4)'s camera, here or mounted
	from another machine) is played as a stream: a reader proc
	consumes framesize-byte chunks sequentially, paced by the SERVER
	(reads park until the next frame; a slow link skips frames, never
	blocks), and the pane blits the newest frame each tick.  The strip
	shows "LIVE <w>x<h> stream"; transport keys are inert.

	LAYERS.  What is drawn over the frames is the feed's layer/
	directory as the composition's name space has it (#1095,
	layers(2)): each file there is a layer of text records (box,
	line, text, point; vision(4)'s detections are boxes as they
	come), drawn bottom first in the union's order.  Composing them
	is the name space's business, done while the pane runs:

	bind -a /mnt/vision/1/layer /mnt/video/0/layer	detections on top
	bind -b /n/remote/mask /mnt/video/0/layer	a layer underneath
	unmount /mnt/vision/1/layer /mnt/video/0/layer	gone

	A bind or unmount shows within half a second.  On frame F each
	layer's records for its newest frame up to F are drawn, and its
	frame=* records.  A live sequential feed names no frames, so on
	one only frame=* records are drawn.  All panes of one Matrix
	share its name space, so they see the same stack of a feed; a
	viewer in another name space sees its own.

